; 34-arity.kurt

; `arity SYMBOL N` declares that `SYMBOL` always takes exactly `N`
; arguments, written by juxtaposition (space, `minimal.kurt`'s built-in
; infix operator) -- this is how ordinary function/predicate symbols like
; `f x y` are declared, as opposed to infix/prefix/postfix operators.

arity f 2

var a, b
parse f a b              ; (f a b)

; Compare this with `doc/kurt-doc.md`'s note on `arity` vs `prefix`: `arity`
; fixes both how many arguments a symbol takes *and* the binding power used
; to grab them (space's), while `prefix` lets you pick a custom binding
; power for a single argument instead.
